@lang en

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `uk-interpretation-time`

-- The Interpretation Act 1978 (1978 c. 30), sections 2, 15, 16 and 17: amendment and repeal in the Session of passing,
-- repeal of a repeal, general savings on repeal, and repeal and re-enactment.
--
-- Source: the converter-made plain-text rendering in ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1978-30.txt, not the official text (see SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
--
-- A boolean named "section N provides that ..." answers FALSE when the section does NOT provide it. FALSE never means that the
-- opposite is the law: the section may be displaced by a contrary intention, or may not reach the case.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE ANSWERS
--   s 2    whether an Act may be amended or repealed in the Session in which it is passed;
--   s 15   whether the repeal of a repealing enactment revives what it had repealed;
--   s 16   what a repeal does not affect, and what may still be done after it as if the repealing Act had not been passed;
--   s 17   how long a repealed enactment remains in force when provisions are substituted, and what a repeal and
--          re-enactment does to references to the old enactment and to things done under it.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE REFUSES, because the text is silent
--   * s 2 says an Act may be amended or repealed in the Session of passing, and is silent on any other Session;
--   * s 16(2) names the expiry of a temporary enactment, and nothing else besides repeal by an Act;
--   * s 17(1), where the substituted provisions have no day on which they come into force that section 4 can give.
--
-- Whether these sections reach a given enactment (Sch 2 paras 1 to 3) is answered by uk-interpretation-application.l4.

§ `Nouns of this module`

DECLARE `Thing that section 16(1) protects` IS ONE OF
    `anything not in force or existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect`
    `the previous operation of the enactment repealed or anything duly done or suffered under it`
    `a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment`
    `a penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of an offence committed against the enactment`
    `an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of such a right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment`

DECLARE `Way an enactment ceases` IS ONE OF
    `repeal by an Act`
    `expiry of a temporary enactment`
    `another way`

-- A repeal and re-enactment (s 17(2)).
DECLARE `A re-enactment` HAS
    `the Act repeals and re-enacts a previous enactment, with or without modification` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contrary intention appears`                                                   IS A BOOLEAN

§ `Named refusals`

-- src:9 | Any Act may be amended or repealed in the Session of Parliament in which it is passed.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 2 (src:9): the section speaks of the Session in which the Act is passed, and of no other
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 2 speaks only of the Session in which the Act is passed and says nothing of another Session` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 2 speaks only of the Session in which the Act is passed and says nothing of another Session"

-- src:70 | (2) This section applies to the expiry of a temporary enactment as if it were repealed by an Act.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 16(2) (src:70): the section names the expiry of a temporary enactment and no other way an enactment ceases besides repeal by an Act
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 16 speaks only of repeal by an Act and of the expiry of a temporary enactment` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 16 speaks only of repeal by an Act and of the expiry of a temporary enactment"

§ `Section 2: amendment or repeal in the same Session`

-- src:8 | 2 Amendment or repeal in same Session.
-- src:9 | Any Act may be amended or repealed in the Session of Parliament in which it is passed.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 2 (src:8-9)
GIVEN `the Session in which the Act is passed` IS A STRING
      `the Session in which it is amended or repealed` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2 permits amendment or repeal in` `the Session in which the Act is passed` `the Session in which it is amended or repealed` MEANS
    IF   `the Session in which the Act is passed` EQUALS `the Session in which it is amended or repealed`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE `section 2 speaks only of the Session in which the Act is passed and says nothing of another Session`

§ `Section 15: repeal of a repeal`

-- src:60 | 15 Repeal of repeal.
-- src:61 | Where an Act repeals a repealing enactment, the repeal does not revive any enactment previously repealed unless words are added reviving it.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 15 (src:60-61)
GIVEN `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 15 provides that the repeal of a repealing enactment does not revive the enactment previously repealed` `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed` MEANS
    NOT `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed`

§ `Section 16: general savings`

-- src:62 | 16 General savings.
-- src:63 | (1) Without prejudice to section 15, where an Act repeals an enactment, the repeal does not, unless the contrary intention appears,—
-- src:64 | (a) revive anything not in force or existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect;
-- src:65 | (b) affect the previous operation of the enactment repealed or anything duly done or suffered under that enactment;
-- src:66 | (c) affect any right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under that enactment;
-- src:67 | (d) affect any penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of any offence committed against that enactment;
-- src:68 | (e) affect any investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of any such right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment;
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 16(1)(a)-(e) (src:62-68)
GIVEN thing IS A `Thing that section 16(1) protects`
      `the contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 16(1) provides that the repeal of an enactment by an Act does not revive or affect` thing `the contrary intention appears` MEANS
    CONSIDER thing
    WHEN `anything not in force or existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`
    WHEN `the previous operation of the enactment repealed or anything duly done or suffered under it` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`
    WHEN `a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`
    WHEN `a penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of an offence committed against the enactment` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`
    WHEN `an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of such a right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`

-- src:69 | and any such investigation, legal proceeding or remedy may be instituted, continued or enforced, and any such penalty, forfeiture or punishment may be imposed, as if the repealing Act had not been passed.
-- "any such investigation, legal proceeding or remedy" and "any such penalty, forfeiture or punishment" are those of (e) and (d).
-- For the other three things the closing words give nothing, so FALSE: the section does not provide that they may be instituted,
-- continued, enforced or imposed.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 16(1), closing words (src:69)
GIVEN thing IS A `Thing that section 16(1) protects`
      `the contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 16(1) provides that it may be instituted, continued, enforced or imposed as if the repealing Act had not been passed` thing `the contrary intention appears` MEANS
    CONSIDER thing
    WHEN `anything not in force or existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the previous operation of the enactment repealed or anything duly done or suffered under it` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of an offence committed against the enactment` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`
    WHEN `an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of such a right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment` THEN NOT `the contrary intention appears`

-- src:70 | (2) This section applies to the expiry of a temporary enactment as if it were repealed by an Act.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 16(2) (src:70)
GIVEN way IS A `Way an enactment ceases`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 16 treats the way the enactment ceases as a repeal by an Act` way MEANS
    CONSIDER way
    WHEN `repeal by an Act` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `expiry of a temporary enactment` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another way` THEN `section 16 speaks only of repeal by an Act and of the expiry of a temporary enactment`

§ `Section 17: repeal and re-enactment`

-- "the repealed enactment remains in force until the substituted provisions come into force".
-- The substituted provisions come into force at the beginning of a day (section 4), so the repealed enactment remains in force
-- on each earlier day, and on the day of that commencement the substituted provisions are already in force from its first moment (fork F12).
-- src:71 | 17 Repeal and re-enactment.
-- src:72 | (1) Where an Act repeals a previous enactment and substitutes provisions for the enactment repealed, the repealed enactment remains in force until the substituted provisions come into force.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 17(1) (src:71-72), read with s 4
GIVEN `the Act repeals a previous enactment and substitutes provisions for it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the substituted provisions` IS AN `An enactment`
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 17(1) provides that the repealed enactment remains in force on` `the Act repeals a previous enactment and substitutes provisions for it` `the substituted provisions` `the day asked about` MEANS
        `the Act repeals a previous enactment and substitutes provisions for it`
    AND `the day asked about` LESS THAN (`section 4 — when the enactment comes into force` `the substituted provisions`)'s `the day`

-- src:73 | (2) Where an Act repeals and re-enacts, with or without modification, a previous enactment then, unless the contrary intention appears,—
-- src:74 | (a) any reference in any other enactment to the enactment so repealed shall be construed as a reference to the provision re-enacted;
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 17(2)(a) (src:73-74)
GIVEN reenactment IS A `A re-enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 17(2)(a) provides that a reference to the repealed enactment is a reference to the provision re-enacted` reenactment MEANS
        reenactment's `the Act repeals and re-enacts a previous enactment, with or without modification`
    AND NOT reenactment's `the contrary intention appears`

-- src:75 | (b) in so far as any subordinate legislation made or other thing done under the enactment so repealed, or having effect as if so made or done, could have been made or done under the provision re-enacted, it shall have effect as if made or done under that provision.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 17(2)(b) (src:75), with the opening words of s 17(2)
GIVEN reenactment IS A `A re-enactment`
      `it could have been made or done under the provision re-enacted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 17(2)(b) provides that the subordinate legislation or thing has effect as if made or done under the provision re-enacted` reenactment `it could have been made or done under the provision re-enacted` MEANS
        reenactment's `the Act repeals and re-enacts a previous enactment, with or without modification`
    AND NOT reenactment's `the contrary intention appears`
    AND `it could have been made or done under the provision re-enacted`
